```php
enum AttributeLifecycle: string
{
    case Draft = 'draft';
    case Active = 'active';
    case Deprecated = 'deprecated';
    case Migrating = 'migrating';
    case Retired = 'retired';
}

final readonly class AttributeId
{
    public function __construct(public string $value) { /* strict UUIDv7 */ }
    public static function generate(): self { return new self((string) Str::uuid7()); }
}

final readonly class AttributeDefinition
{
    /** @param list<string> $readKeys @param list<string> $writeKeys */
    public function __construct(
        public AttributeId $id,
        public string $entity,
        public string $key,
        public int $revision,
        public string $type,
        public AttributeLifecycle $lifecycle,
        public bool $required,
        public bool $searchable,
        public array $constraints,
        public array $readKeys,
        public array $writeKeys,
        public IndexPolicy $index,
        public array $meta = [],
    ) {}
}
```

`revision >= 1`. Canonical hash serialises sorted maps and definitions sorted by
`id`, then revision. `FieldSnapshot` keeps all five constructor arguments and its
legacy `activeFields`; each row additively carries `id`, `key`, `revision`,
`lifecycle`, `constraints`, `read_keys`, `write_keys`, `index`. `code` remains alias
of `key` through P15. `schemaVersion = 'attributes/2'`; `schemaHash` is SHA-256.

```php
interface AttributeStoreResolver
{
    public function forEntity(string $entity): AttributeStore;
}

final readonly class AttributeStoragePolicy
{
    /** @param array<string,'jsonb'|'eav'> $entities */
    public function __construct(public string $default, public array $entities) {}
}
```

`ResolvingAttributeStore` preserves the existing `AttributeStore` binding. For a
single record it delegates by `RecordRef::entity`; `loadMany` partitions records by
entity/driver, delegates, then restores input order. Unknown driver, mixed identity
or unavailable DB fails closed. Existing global `storage_driver` becomes default;
`storage_by_entity` is additive. Per-field choice is invalid.

`IndexPolicy` modes:

- `none`: default, no schema operation;
- `expression_btree`: only measured equality/range/order shape, typed cast, optional
  static partial predicate; query AST must emit byte-equivalent expression/predicate;
- `promoted`: canonical value remains attributes-owned; subject package owns its
  typed projection column and subscribes externally. Attributes never alters it.

Before an expression index: validate every existing value can cast, store measurement
and `EXPLAIN` before/after receipt, create concurrently through existing queue, verify,
then activate policy revision. Index name derives from attribute id + revision, not key.
Ordering adds record id as deterministic tie-breaker. A broad GIN index is outside P10.

The guard executes in this order and returns internal reason codes:

1. field exists in authoritative snapshot;
2. expected schema revision/hash is compatible;
3. tenant scope owns subject (`DataScopeAuthorizer`, action `attributes.subject`);
4. actor can view field (`attributes.view`);
5. actor can write field (`attributes.write`);
6. current operation can mutate it (`attributes.mutate`);
7. value matches declared type;
8. value satisfies constraints (required, enum, length/range, format/reference).

The existing authorizer contract is reused; resource is a canonical opaque string
containing entity, record id and stable attribute id, never a PHP class. Batch denial
is atomic. External exception is one `ScopeDenied`/`InvalidAttributeValue` family
without revealing existence; logs/receipts keep the distinct reason. P13 later binds
operation-aware policy to action `attributes.mutate`; P10 does not invent `onlyFields`.
